@node Timely Core Concepts
@section A first dataflow

Here is about the simplest non-trivial timely dataflow program: it
streams the numbers 0 through 9 and inspects each one.

@example
static bool
range_next(void *ctx, dtfl_emit *out)
@{
        uint64_t *next = ctx;
        unsigned char buf[8];

        if (*next >= 10)
                return false;
        dtfl_put_u64(buf, *next);
        dtfl_emit_record(out, buf, sizeof buf);
        (*next)++;
        return true;
@}

static void
inspect_seen(void *ctx, const uint64_t *time, const void *rec, size_t len)
@{
        printf("seen: %llu\n", (unsigned long long)dtfl_get_u64(rec));
@}

static void
build(dtfl_scope *scope, void *ctx)
@{
        dtfl_stream *s = dtfl_to_stream(scope, dtfl_type_u64(),
            range_next, DTFL_CTX(ctx, NULL));

        dtfl_stream_inspect(s, inspect_seen, DTFL_NOCTX);
@}
@end example

A worker runs this build function inside @code{dtfl_worker_dataflow},
then steps until the dataflow is done:

@example
static void
worker(dtfl_worker *w, void *ctx)
@{
        dtfl_time_class *tc = dtfl_time_u64();
        uint64_t next = 0;

        dtfl_worker_dataflow(w, "hello", tc, build, &next);
        while (dtfl_worker_step(w))
                ;
        dtfl_time_class_free(tc);
@}

int
main(void)
@{
        return dtfl_execute_directly(worker, NULL);
@}
@end example

@code{dtfl_execute_directly} runs one worker on the calling thread.  The
complete program is in @file{doc/snippets/timely_hello.c}; it prints:

@example
seen: 0
seen: 1
@dots{}
seen: 9
@end example

Note how the C API renders two Rust idioms.  A Rust closure
(@code{|x| println!(@dots{})}) becomes a function pointer and a context:
@code{inspect_seen} plus @code{DTFL_NOCTX} (no context).  Rust's
iterator (@code{(0..10).to_stream(scope)}) becomes @code{dtfl_to_stream}
driven by a @code{range_next} callback that emits one record at a time
and returns @code{false} at the end.  Because a record in libdtfl is a
byte string, @code{dtfl_put_u64} and @code{dtfl_get_u64} move native
values in and out of the eight-byte @code{u64} record (@pxref{Records}).

@subsection A reactive example

A dataflow is more than a loop because it can be @emph{reactive}: run it
without knowing the data ahead of time, and feed data in as the
computation proceeds.  The next program creates an input, exchanges each
record across workers by its value, inspects it, and uses a probe to
keep the input and the dataflow in step.

@example
static uint64_t
route_by_value(const void *rec, size_t len, void *ctx)
@{
        return dtfl_get_u64(rec);
@}

static void
build(dtfl_scope *scope, void *arg)
@{
        build_ctx *c = arg;
        dtfl_stream *s = dtfl_input_to_stream(c->input, scope);

        s = dtfl_stream_exchange(s, route_by_value, NULL);
        s = dtfl_stream_inspect(s, say_hello, DTFL_NOCTX);
        dtfl_stream_probe_with(s, c->probe);
@}
@end example

The driver introduces one record per round and advances the input's
timestamp after each:

@example
for (round = 0; round < 10; round++) @{
        uint64_t next = round + 1;
        unsigned char buf[8];

        dtfl_put_u64(buf, round);
        dtfl_input_send(c->input, buf, sizeof buf);
        dtfl_input_advance_to(c->input, &next);
        while (dtfl_probe_less_than(c->probe, dtfl_input_time(c->input)))
                dtfl_worker_step(w);
@}
dtfl_input_close(c->input);
@end example

The complete program is in @file{doc/snippets/timely_input.c}.  Run on
one worker it prints @code{hello 0} through @code{hello 9} in order.
Run on several workers (configure the mode and thread count through
@code{dtfl_config}, @pxref{Installation}), each record is routed to the
worker its value hashes to, so the lines interleave; the next section
explains why that is fine, and how the probe keeps the output sensible.
